 PART II — TECHNICAL DEEP DIVE AND REPRODUCIBLE LAB
Supplement to the original article — original content retained in full
17. How to use this supplement
This supplement extends the original article with deeper implementation notes, a more complete local lab, a small sequence-analysis example, evaluation guidance, and a specialist glossary. The original 15-page article is preserved verbatim before this section. The code below is intended for systems you own or are explicitly authorised to test. It does not attempt to bypass third-party anti-bot controls.
Important reproducibility rule: no experiment results are asserted here as if they had already been measured. Run the lab in your own environment, record the exact versions and outputs, and then add your observations to the worksheet in Section 20.
18. Deeper protocol details that matter in practice
18.1 TLS ClientHello: what is and is not observable
A TLS ClientHello can contain the offered protocol versions, cipher suites, extension types, supported groups, signature algorithms, key-share information, and other parameters. Fingerprinting systems select and normalise some of these fields; they do not usually preserve every byte of the handshake as a universal client identity. A fingerprint is a derived feature whose meaning depends on the collection point, normalisation method, and population against which it is interpreted.
A common engineering mistake is to assume an application handler can reconstruct the original ClientHello. After a TLS terminator accepts a connection, the application usually receives decrypted HTTP metadata, not the raw handshake. If a CDN, reverse proxy, service mesh, or TLS inspection appliance terminates TLS first, the origin may observe that intermediary's connection. A production design should document the exact observation point and securely pass only the needed, provenance-labelled metadata downstream.
•	JA3 traditionally derives a compact identifier from selected ClientHello fields; GREASE values are excluded from the canonicalisation process.
•	JA4 is a related fingerprinting family with a different, more structured representation. JA3 and JA4 values are not directly interchangeable.
•	TLS 1.3 encrypts most handshake messages after ServerHello, but the ClientHello itself is visible to an on-path observer unless additional protections such as Encrypted ClientHello are in use.
•	A shared fingerprint can reflect a common browser build, library, proxy, or deployment—not necessarily a shared person, device, or intent.
For exact algorithms and current implementation details, use the primary JA3/JA4 project documentation and relevant TLS specifications listed in the original references. Do not implement a partial fingerprinting algorithm and label it JA3-compatible unless it follows the documented canonicalisation precisely.
18.2 HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3 are different observation surfaces
Header ordering is easiest to discuss for HTTP/1.1, where field lines are transmitted as text. HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 use binary framing and pseudo-headers; compression and protocol libraries affect what an application-level logger sees. An application framework may expose a normalised mapping rather than the original wire sequence. Therefore, a header-order experiment using Python's BaseHTTPRequestHandler can demonstrate what that handler receives over HTTP/1.1, but it cannot establish wire-level ordering for HTTP/2 or HTTP/3.
Layer	Useful observations	Common blind spot
TLS terminator / edge	Negotiated TLS version, cipher, ALPN, handshake-derived fingerprint where supported	May see a proxy or CDN connection rather than the end client
HTTP parser	Method, target, fields, protocol version as exposed by the parser	Frameworks may normalise fields or discard wire-level details
Application	Route, authentication state, session transitions, response outcomes	Usually cannot infer the original TLS ClientHello by itself
Browser-side instrumentation	JavaScript state, feature results, challenge completion, client-side timing	Automation can execute JavaScript; privacy settings can change results
Analytics pipeline	Correlated sequences and rolling aggregates	Bad identifiers or unsynchronised clocks can corrupt joins

18.3 Header semantics versus fingerprints
A field's meaning comes from its protocol definition and request context, not merely its position. For example, Fetch Metadata fields such as Sec-Fetch-Site, Sec-Fetch-Mode, and Sec-Fetch-Dest can help describe the context of a browser request. They are not universal requirements for all HTTP clients, and their absence is not proof of abuse. Similarly, Accept and Accept-Language can vary because of user preferences, privacy tools, browser versions, or the type of request.
A sound detector compares like with like: top-level navigation with expected navigation patterns, API calls with the API's documented contract, and authenticated actions with the relevant session state. This reduces the risk of treating an approved integration as suspicious merely because it is not a browser.
19. Reproducible local lab: improved implementation
This lab records request metadata on loopback only. It is deliberately small enough to inspect. It does not collect TLS ClientHello data, emulate a third-party service, or try to evade a detector.
19.1 Requirements
•	Python 3.10 or later is recommended.
•	Install the HTTP client dependency with: python -m pip install requests
•	For the browser example, install Playwright with: python -m pip install playwright, then run: python -m playwright install chromium
•	Use a terminal for the server and a second terminal for the client. Keep the server bound to 127.0.0.1.
19.2 Local observation server — observer.py
This version adds a timestamp and a request identifier, uses a threaded server for simple concurrent local tests, and returns JSON. It records only what the HTTP server receives. Do not expose this demonstration server to an untrusted network.
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer
import json
import uuid
 
class Observer(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    server_version = "LocalObserver/1.0"
 
    def do_GET(self):
        record = {
            "request_id": str(uuid.uuid4()),
            "observed_at_utc": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(),
            "client_address": self.client_address[0],
            "method": self.command,
            "path": self.path,
            "http_version": self.request_version,
            # This is the header representation exposed by Python's parser,
            # not a guaranteed byte-for-byte capture of the wire.
            "headers": list(self.headers.items()),
        }
 
        print(json.dumps(record, indent=2))
        body = json.dumps({
            "ok": True,
            "request_id": record["request_id"],
            "header_count": len(record["headers"]),
            "note": "Local metadata only; no TLS fingerprint is captured."
        }).encode("utf-8")
 
        self.send_response(200)
        self.send_header("Content-Type", "application/json; charset=utf-8")
        self.send_header("Content-Length", str(len(body)))
        self.end_headers()
        self.wfile.write(body)
 
    def log_message(self, fmt, *args):
        # Structured records above are easier to compare in this lab.
        return
 
if __name__ == "__main__":
    server = ThreadingHTTPServer(("127.0.0.1", 8080), Observer)
    print("Listening only on http://127.0.0.1:8080")
    try:
        server.serve_forever()
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        print("\\nStopping local observer.")
    finally:
        server.server_close()
19.3 Ordinary requests client — requests_client.py
import requests
 
url = "http://127.0.0.1:8080/demo?source=requests"
response = requests.get(url, timeout=5)
 
print("Status:", response.status_code)
print("Response:", response.json())
print("Client-side request headers:")
for name, value in response.request.headers.items():
    print(f"{name}: {value}")
Run the server first with python observer.py, then run python requests_client.py in another terminal. The server-side record is the main evidence for this comparison. The client-side printout is useful context, but it should not be confused with a packet capture.
19.4 Browser request — browser_client.py
from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright
 
with sync_playwright() as p:
    browser = p.chromium.launch(headless=True)
    page = browser.new_page()
    response = page.goto("http://127.0.0.1:8080/demo?source=playwright",
                         wait_until="domcontentloaded")
    print("Status:", response.status if response else "No main response")
    print("Page title:", page.title())
    browser.close()
Run this script while the observer is active. Because the endpoint returns JSON rather than an HTML page, the browser may display the JSON response directly; that is expected. The main navigation request is the request to compare. A headless browser is still a browser runtime, but its traffic is not guaranteed to be identical to a user's everyday browser session.
19.5 Capture a clean experiment record
8.	Record the operating system, Python version, requests version, Playwright version, and browser version.
9.	Start the observer and save its console output to a text file.
10.	Send one requests request and one browser navigation request.
11.	Repeat each request a few times to see which fields remain stable and which vary.
12.	Change only one variable at a time, such as browser headless versus headed mode, and label each run.
13.	Do not generalise from two requests to all Python clients or all browsers.
Version commands: python --version; python -m pip show requests; python -m pip show playwright. For Playwright browser information, record the installed Playwright version and the browser binary version reported by your local environment.
20. Results worksheet and interpretation discipline
Complete this table after running the experiment. Leave fields blank until measured; do not copy sample values from another machine. Preserve raw output privately if it contains information you do not want to publish.
Field	requests client	Browser client	Interpretation / caveat
Run ID and timestamp			Use the same clock source and label each run.
Python / library / browser versions			Version differences can change results.
User-Agent			Self-declared label; not identity proof.
Accept			Depends on request type and client defaults.
Accept-Encoding			Compression support can vary by client and runtime.
Accept-Language			May reflect user or browser preferences.
Sec-Fetch-* fields			Contextual browser metadata; not expected from every client.
Header count / observed order			Order is only what the chosen parser exposes.
HTTP version reported			The local server demonstrates HTTP/1.1, not HTTP/2 or HTTP/3.
Cookies / redirects			Test only if you add a controlled local workflow.
Unexpected result			Record it rather than forcing it to match an expectation.

20.1 Example of sound interpretation
Suppose the browser request contains more fields than the requests request. A defensible finding is: 'In this run, the local server observed additional fields in the browser navigation request.' A plausible interpretation is that the browser's navigation stack added context that the basic HTTP client did not send. An indefensible conclusion would be: 'The browser is human and requests is a bot.' The experiment measures request construction, not intent, authorisation, or human presence.
21. A small, transparent sequence-risk demonstration
The following example shows how a service could aggregate several weak signals into a transparent score. It uses synthetic, illustrative events and is not a production detector. Thresholds are arbitrary placeholders that must not be copied into a live service without validation. The example deliberately avoids treating a single IP, User-Agent, or request-rate observation as proof of abuse.
21.1 Synthetic events and a simple scoring function
from collections import Counter
from dataclasses import dataclass
from typing import Iterable
 
@dataclass
class Event:
    session_id: str
    route: str
    status: int
    seconds_since_previous: float
    has_valid_session: bool
 
def score_session(events: Iterable[Event]) -> tuple[int, list[str]]:
    events = list(events)
    reasons = []
    score = 0
 
    if not events:
        return 0, ["no events to evaluate"]
 
    # These are illustrative signals, not universal rules.
    rapid = sum(e.seconds_since_previous < 0.25 for e in events[1:])
    if rapid >= 5:
        score += 2
        reasons.append("repeated very short inter-request intervals")
 
    invalid_responses = sum(400 <= e.status < 500 for e in events)
    if invalid_responses >= 4:
        score += 2
        reasons.append("repeated client-error responses")
 
    invalid_session = sum(not e.has_valid_session for e in events)
    if invalid_session >= 3:
        score += 3
        reasons.append("repeated requests without expected session state")
 
    route_counts = Counter(e.route for e in events)
    if len(events) >= 10 and len(route_counts) == 1:
        score += 1
        reasons.append("highly repetitive route sequence")
 
    return score, reasons
 
if __name__ == "__main__":
    sample = [
        Event("demo-1", "/catalog", 200, 1.4, True),
        Event("demo-1", "/catalog", 200, 0.18, True),
        Event("demo-1", "/catalog", 404, 0.16, False),
        Event("demo-1", "/catalog", 404, 0.14, False),
        Event("demo-1", "/catalog", 404, 0.15, False),
        Event("demo-1", "/catalog", 404, 0.17, False),
        Event("demo-1", "/catalog", 200, 0.12, False),
    ]
    score, reasons = score_session(sample)
    print("Illustrative score:", score)
    print("Reasons:", reasons)
    print("Decision: review the context; do not auto-block on this score alone.")
This code is intentionally simple enough to audit. In a real service, event collection would need robust session identifiers, clock handling, route-specific expectations, safe logging, and protection against missing or duplicated events. The score should be calibrated on representative legitimate and abusive traffic, and the enforcement action should depend on endpoint sensitivity and the cost of a false positive.
21.2 Why a score needs calibration
•	Precision answers: of requests flagged, what proportion were actually abusive?
•	Recall answers: of abusive requests, what proportion did the detector identify?
•	False-positive rate answers: of legitimate requests, what proportion were incorrectly flagged?
•	Calibration asks whether predicted risk levels correspond to observed outcome frequencies.
•	Shadow mode lets engineers compare a proposed rule with real traffic without enforcing it immediately.
•	Segment results by endpoint, client family, network type, and release period to uncover uneven impact.
A higher score is not automatically a better detector. If the labels used for evaluation come only from an earlier detector, the new system can inherit the old system's mistakes. Use independent evidence such as confirmed abuse, analyst review, appeal outcomes, and carefully designed test traffic where available.
22. Advanced failure modes and niche engineering considerations
22.1 Fingerprint drift and population effects
Fingerprints are population-dependent. A fingerprint may be common in one geography or enterprise fleet and rare in another. Browser rollouts can shift distributions quickly, while a proxy deployment can make many clients appear to share one transport profile. Store the observation time, collection point, and relevant version context so analysts can distinguish a real change in traffic from an instrumentation change.
22.2 Correlated signals and double-counting
A risk model can become overconfident when several features measure nearly the same underlying fact. For example, a library-specific User-Agent, a familiar header set, and a known TLS profile may all be consequences of the same client implementation. Treating them as three independent pieces of evidence exaggerates confidence. Evaluate feature correlation, document causal assumptions, and test whether each feature adds value beyond existing signals.
22.3 Clock skew, retries, and event ordering
Sequence analysis depends on trustworthy timestamps and stable correlation identifiers. Events from different services may arrive out of order; retries can create duplicate records; queues can delay logs; and clock skew can make intervals negative or implausible. Prefer monotonic clocks for durations measured within one process, synchronised wall clocks for cross-system correlation, explicit request IDs, and deduplication rules. Preserve missingness rather than silently replacing unknown values with zero.
22.4 NAT, proxy chains, and identity scope
A source address identifies a network observation, not necessarily a person or even one client. Carrier-grade NAT, campus networks, VPN gateways, corporate proxies, and CDNs can aggregate unrelated traffic. If the system trusts forwarded headers, it must accept them only from known proxy hops and define which proxy is authoritative; blindly trusting a client-supplied X-Forwarded-For value can let a requester forge apparent origin addresses.
22.5 Challenge success is not proof of benign intent
A successful JavaScript challenge indicates that the required computation or interaction completed in the observed environment. It does not prove a human was present or that subsequent activity is authorised. Conversely, challenge failure may be caused by script blocking, network errors, accessibility tooling, or privacy protections. Treat challenge outcomes as one event in the session history, and provide a recovery path for legitimate users.
22.6 Rate limits should match the resource
A single global threshold ignores differences between cheap reads, expensive searches, login attempts, bulk exports, and state-changing actions. Token-bucket and leaky-bucket algorithms are useful rate-control primitives, but their keys and budgets should reflect the threat model: account, API key, session, endpoint, network, or combinations. Distributed clients can spread load across keys, while shared networks can make IP-only budgets unfair.
22.7 Privacy and data minimisation
Fingerprinting data can become linkable when retained or joined across sessions. Before collecting a feature, document the security purpose, who can access it, retention duration, and whether a less granular feature would work. Avoid logging authentication secrets, full cookies, session tokens, or unnecessary request bodies in a demonstration. For public repositories, use synthetic data and remove environment-specific identifiers.
